# Keep the build context down to what the Dockerfile actually reads.
#
# The Dockerfile only ever COPYs `web/` and `backend/`, and both are thrown away
# again after their builder stage, so nothing else has to reach the daemon.
# Without this file the context is the whole repo (~955 MB, dominated by
# node_modules/.next/.git); BuildKit hashes and uploads all of it whenever its
# cache is cold — a fresh clone, a CI runner, or a different machine.
#
# The rule for adding entries: exclude anything the build does not read. If you
# later add a COPY/ADD for one of these paths, delete the entry.

# --- secrets and runtime-only state ------------------------------------------
# These are bind-mounted at runtime by docker-compose.yaml (./.env:/app/.env,
# ./certs:/app/certs), never baked into the image. Keeping them out of the
# context means the Redis password and the TLS/LiveKit keys are not shipped to
# the daemon on every build.
.env
.env.*
certs

# --- generated output, rebuilt inside the image ------------------------------
# frontend-builder runs `npm ci && npm run build` from source, so the host's
# node_modules (591 MB), .next (201 MB) and out (21 MB) are all stale copies.
web/node_modules
web/.next
web/out
**/node_modules
**/.next

# TypeScript incremental-build state, produced by `npm run type-check`; it is
# gitignored, and `npm run build` inside the image writes its own.
web/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# --- large source assets not needed to build ---------------------------------
# web/assets-src holds the original artwork plus the commands that regenerate
# public/bg-*.webp; the build consumes the WebP files, not the sources.
web/assets-src

# --- scratch / local-only ----------------------------------------------------
.git
tmp
backend/tmp
.claude
.vscode

# --- editor and OS noise -----------------------------------------------------
*.log
**/.DS_Store
